president Trump's former campaign  chairman Paul Manafort is in federal  court for the start of his high-stakes  criminal trial this marks a new public  phase in Special Counsel Robert Muller's  14-month Russia investigation Manafort  faces bank fraud and tax evasion charges  tied to his lobbying work for a  russia-backed political party in Ukraine  if convicted he could serve life in  prison Paula Reed is outside the court  house in Alexandria Virginia with how  the Russia investigation looms over this  trial Paula good morning good morning  these stakes could not be higher for  President Trump or Special Counsel  robert muller as jury selection gets  underway here at federal court in  virginia now there is nothing in these  charges about Russian interference in  the campaign but this case is the first  public test for Special Counsel Robert  Muller he had been hoping that mana fort  would flip and share what he knows about  any Russian involvement in the campaign  but we spoke to Manta Ford's lawyer just  moments ago as he entered court here for  the first day of this trial he told me  there is no chance that manda fort will  strike a last-minute deal to avoid trial  now this case will involve 35 witnesses  and more than 500 pieces of evidence in  an attempt to prove that mana fort  earned tens of millions of dollars from  his work with Ukrainian officials they  also allege that he hid a significant  percentage of that money from the IRS  and spend it on expensive homes cars and  even clothes as a way to launder that  money now one of the witnesses expected  to testify is former Deputy Trump  campaign Chairman Richard Gates who is  mana forts one-time business partner now  sources tell me that mana port is  banking on a presidential pardon if he  is convicted during this trial Paula  thank you
 

